    "### 6. Data Modelling\n",
    "\n",
    "The three models form a nested sequence tied directly to the research question. Model 1 estimates the overall association between in-state tuition and median earnings. Model 2 adds institutional sector and tuition-by-sector interactions. Model 3 tests whether those relationships remain after adjustment for undergraduate enrollment, predominant degree, and state.\n",
    "\n",
    "This design follows institution-level higher-education research rather than treating the models as causal estimates. Faber and Slantcheva-Durst (2021, pp. 687–700) model earnings using College Scorecard institutional attributes. The open dissertation underlying that article defines earnings as an institution-level outcome, groups state, enrollment, and tuition among the explanatory attributes, and uses OLS to estimate their relationship with earnings (Faber, 2017, pp. 41–45). Muse and Muse (2024, pp. 36–40) likewise use College Scorecard earnings with tuition, institutional type, size, degree offerings, geography, and selectivity controls.\n",
    "\n",
    "Natural logarithms are applied to tuition and earnings because both are positive and right-skewed. The log-log coefficients are elasticities, so a coefficient of 0.10 means that a 1% increase in tuition is associated with approximately 0.10% higher earnings. Muse and Muse (2024, pp. 37, 40) also model the natural logarithm of College Scorecard median earnings and explain how the transformation reduces positive skewness. Enrollment uses `log1p` because one institution reports zero undergraduate enrollment.\n",
    "\n",
    "All models use HC3 heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors. The Breusch-Pagan diagnostics below reject constant residual variance, while MacKinnon and White (1985, pp. 307–309) derive the jackknife-based HC3 covariance estimator for regression inference under heteroskedasticity. The estimates therefore describe conditional associations and must not be interpreted as tuition causing earnings."
   ]
  },
