   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 6a. Model 1. Overall tuition and earnings relationship\n",
    "\n",
    "Model 1 estimates a positive tuition elasticity of 0.121 with a 95% confidence interval from 0.113 to 0.129 and `p < .001`. A 1% increase in in-state tuition is therefore associated with approximately 0.121% higher median earnings across the full sample. The model explains 18.7% of the variation in log earnings, meaning 81.3% of earnings differences across institutions are unrelated to tuition. The Breusch-Pagan test rejects homoskedasticity, which supports the use of HC3 standard errors rather than the conventional OLS covariance estimate.\n",
    "\n",
    "Translating to practical terms, a student comparing a public institution charging $6,000 against a private nonprofit charging $34,000 faces roughly a fivefold tuition difference. The model predicts about a 0.9% earnings advantage for the higher-tuition institution, or roughly $400 on a $45,000 median starting salary. The unconditional relationship is statistically reliable but economically modest.\n",
    "\n",
    "The residual diagnostics point to two additional concerns. The Q-Q plot shows heavier tails than a normal distribution, and the residual scatter widens at higher fitted values. HC3 corrects for heteroskedasticity but not for non-normality in the tails. For a sample of 2,894 institutions this is manageable, but it weakens the precision of inference for extreme observations and for the smaller for-profit sector.\n",
    "\n",
    "The coefficient is an unconditional association. Sector composition, degree level, enrollment, geography, student selection, field of study, and local labor markets can all contribute to the observed relationship. Faber (2017, pp. 43–46) similarly warns that institution-level correlational models do not establish cause and effect and remain limited by omitted labor-market and student-preparation variables.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 6b. Model 2. Sector interaction model\n",
    "\n",
    "Model 2 adds public, private nonprofit, and for-profit indicators and interacts each sector with log tuition. Public institutions are the reference group. The joint HC3 Wald test evaluates whether the two interaction coefficients equal zero. Rejecting that hypothesis means the tuition and earnings slope differs by sector, which directly answers the second part of the research question.\n",
    "\n",
    "Institutional control is substantively relevant rather than a cosmetic grouping. Muse and Muse (2024, pp. 36, 39) distinguish the same three control types and include institutional type when modelling College Scorecard earnings. Faber and Slantcheva-Durst (2021, pp. 687–700) likewise show that institutional attributes change the interpretation of tuition and earnings relationships."
   ]
  },
