   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Model 2 raises adjusted R-squared from 0.187 to 0.397, more than doubling the explained variance. Adding sector and its interaction with tuition accounts for roughly 21 percentage points of earnings variation that tuition alone could not explain. The log RMSE drops from 0.236 to 0.203, and the AIC falls from -153 to -1013, confirming the substantial improvement in fit.\n",
    "\n",
    "The estimated tuition elasticities are 0.194 for public institutions (95% CI [0.176, 0.213]), 0.277 for private nonprofits (95% CI [0.242, 0.312]), and 0.400 for for-profits (95% CI [0.301, 0.499]). Each slope is positive with `p < .001`. The joint Wald test for the interaction terms yields a statistic of 30.122 with `p < .001`, confirming that the tuition-earnings relationship differs across sectors.\n",
    "\n",
    "The range is practically meaningful. A student comparing a $15,000 tuition at a for-profit against a $15,000 tuition at a public institution would, by the Model 2 estimates, expect roughly twice the earnings increment at the for-profit. But this comparison is misleading. The high for-profit elasticity reflects the fact that for-profit institutions cluster in particular states, enroll smaller and more vocationally oriented student bodies, and offer a different degree mix. Their apparent sensitivity to tuition is a composition effect, not a genuine sector advantage.\n",
    "\n",
    "These estimates do not imply that increasing tuition would raise earnings. The interaction model still combines tuition with institutional differences that vary systematically across sectors. Model 3 tests whether the sector pattern survives adjustment for observable institutional structure.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 6c. Model 3. Institutional-control robustness model\n",
    "\n",
    "Model 3 retains the Model 2 interaction and adds three prespecified control blocks. Undergraduate enrollment enters as `log1p(UGDS)`, predominant degree enters categorically with bachelor's degree as the reference, and institution state enters as fixed effects. Guam, Puerto Rico, and the U.S. Virgin Islands are grouped as `US Territory` to avoid separate fixed effects supported by only one or two observations.\n",
    "\n",
    "The control set follows the closest published precedents that can be mapped honestly to this dataset. Faber (2017, pp. 42–45) includes state, enrollment, and tuition among blocked College Scorecard institutional attributes before estimating earnings with OLS. Muse and Muse (2024, pp. 36–40) include selectivity, region, institutional type, size, degree offerings, tuition, and log earnings. Graduate debt is excluded from the primary model because it may lie on the pathway from tuition to later outcomes. Admission rate is handled separately because it is observed for only 50.7% of the cleaned sample.\n",
    "\n",
    "The robustness question is whether the sector-specific tuition elasticities remain positive and whether the interaction terms remain jointly significant after these controls. A joint HC3 Wald test also evaluates whether the additional institutional controls contribute explanatory information."
   ]
  },
