   "source": [
    "Model 3 explains 59.1% of the variation in log earnings, with an adjusted R-squared of 0.582. The added institutional controls are jointly significant with Wald p < 10^-265. The log RMSE falls to 0.178 from 0.203 in Model 2. The Breusch-Pagan result remains significant, so HC3 inference remains necessary.\n",
    "\n",
    "After adjustment, the tuition elasticities are 0.189 for public institutions (95% CI [0.168, 0.210]), 0.147 for private nonprofits (95% CI [0.116, 0.178]), and 0.119 for for-profits (95% CI [0.034, 0.205]). The public and private nonprofit estimates remain at `p < .001`, while the for-profit estimate drops to `p = .006`. The joint tuition-by-sector test yields `p = .0465`, falling just below the conventional 0.05 threshold.\n",
    "\n",
    "The central result is the collapse of the for-profit elasticity from 0.400 in Model 2 to 0.119 in Model 3. Adding enrollment, degree level, and state fixed effects accounts for roughly 70% of the unadjusted for-profit coefficient, bringing it below the public estimate. The most plausible explanation is that for-profit institutions are concentrated in states with lower earnings baselines, offer predominantly sub-baccalaureate credentials, and enroll smaller student bodies, all of which suppress earnings independently of tuition. Once those factors are held constant, the for-profit sector shows the weakest tuition-earnings link, not the strongest.\n",
    "\n",
    "The remaining sector differences are narrow in practical terms. The three elasticities span a range of 0.07 percentage points. For a student facing a $10,000 tuition difference between two institutions, the predicted earnings gap is $189 at a public institution, $147 at a nonprofit, and $119 at a for-profit. Whether a range of $70 separates meaningful policy options is a judgment the data cannot make.\n",
    "\n",
    "The interaction p-value of 0.0465 is marginal. The sector differences that survive adjustment, while statistically detectable, are substantially smaller than the unadjusted estimates implied. The controls themselves explain roughly 19 percentage points of additional variance beyond Model 2, suggesting that where an institution is located, how many students it enrolls, and what degrees it awards matter more for earnings than the tuition price itself. A student choosing between institutions may derive more information from state of residence and degree type than from the tuition figure.\n",
    "\n",
    "The model remains descriptive. State fixed effects absorb average differences across state labor markets, but aggregate College Scorecard data cannot control fully for student background, major mix, local occupation, or selection into institutions. Faber (2017, pp. 45–47) identifies the same causal and omitted-variable boundaries for institution-level College Scorecard regressions.\n"
   ]
  },
