   "source": [
    "Within the admission-reporting subset, adding admission rate leaves the public and private nonprofit tuition elasticities positive and significant. Their estimates are 0.175 and 0.128, respectively, both with `p < .001`. The for-profit estimate is -0.044 with `p = .823`, and its confidence interval is wide because only 55 reporting for-profit institutions remain. The sector interactions are not jointly significant in this restricted model with `p = .155`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Admission rate itself has a coefficient of -0.103 with `p < .001`, conditional on the other controls. The sensitivity analysis therefore supports the main tuition result for public and private nonprofit institutions but cannot validate it for the underrepresented for-profit subset. Missing admission rates are not random, so this restricted result must not be generalized to all institutions. Muse and Muse (2024, pp. 35–37) document the same coverage problem for admissions measures and use explicit missingness handling in their broader dataset."
   ]
